+## Why YES (resource remit)
+The text is the most honest cost-control design yet put to Parliament. §3(1) calls ceilings accounting/dispatch ceilings, not a mathematical per-request billing cap, and names the 20-second OpenCode poller as stop-on-detection. §3(1)(a)–(b) hold zero-billed and provider-billed dispatch until an independently verified runtime/account control or provider-enforced ceiling exists on the exact path; a `-free` name is not enforcement. §3(1)(c) requires numeric per-request/continuation/concurrency/CPU/wall ceilings and an overrun reported as a number, never zero. §3(2) keeps the 45-day cumulative whole-host `UPPER_BOUND` non-resettable by restart, rename or expansion — the anti-chaining safeguard I defended in act32 — with fail-closed wind-down. §3(5) requires a measured four-office envelope with a `NEEDS-FOUNDER:` escape, so no office activates on a knowingly inadequate budget. The parallel, no-prelaunch-gate design matches the newest exact-author Founder direction, and §7 supersedes the blocking enacted clauses under Acts 6/31 while preserving the Constitution and independent checks.
+
+## Falsifiers that would flip this vote to NO
+A different digest on the balloted file without fresh genuine review; weakening or resetting `UPPER_BOUND`; zero-billed admission degraded to a name-only match or removal of the verified provider ceiling; reintroduction of a serial first-sample, value/calibration proof or pre-start PROCEED gate; removal of the assumptions schedule or corrected sponsor response from `main`; absence of aggregate ceilings so four concurrent offices can exhaust the host; or any claim that this vote enacts, activates or proves an office's value.
